@node	0.5, 0.6, 0.4, Chapter 0
@section Mutation and ownership

This section covers the language's mutable cell, aliasing, and identity.
Kotlin has neither Rust's ownership rules nor OCaml's ref cells as separate
beings: the mutable cell is a @code{var}, and the interesting questions are
when this book permits one and what it costs.

@heading The captured @code{var}

A @code{var} declared in a function body is one mutable cell, and a lambda
that mentions it closes over the cell itself, not a copy. Two closures
made by the same call share one cell; two calls make two cells. That is
exactly the mechanism section @ref{3.1} exists to teach, which is why this
edition writes it with a plain @code{var} rather than hiding the cell
inside a class:

@example kotlin
public fun makeCounter(start: Int): () -> Int @{
    var n = start
    return @{
        n += 1
        n
    @}
@}

public fun makeWithdrawer(balance: Long): (Long) -> Long @{
    var b = balance
    return @{ amount ->
        b -= amount
        b
    @}
@}

val c1 = makeCounter(0)
val c2 = makeCounter(0)
c1()
// => 1

c1()
// => 2

c2()
// => 1

val w = makeWithdrawer(100L)
w(30L)
// => 70

w(20L)
// => 50
@end example

@noindent
The house rule follows from this: a @code{val} everywhere is the default,
and a captured @code{var} appears only where the book itself assigns, which
in this edition means the closure state of @ref{3.1} and @ref{3.2}, the
simulator internals of chapters 4 and 5, and the one table and queue
implementation of @ref{3.3}. Everywhere else the listings are
assignment-free.

@heading Aliasing and identity

Mutating one cell is visible through every name that holds it. The
runtime's mutable pair @code{VPair} is the book's mutable pair of
@ref{3.3.1}: @code{cons} builds it, and @code{setCar} and @code{setCdr}
mutate the one cell that every alias sees. Because a mutable pair is
identified by which object it is, the runtime's pairs compare with @code{==}
by identity, and @code{===} traces the aliasing directly:

@example kotlin
val p = cons(VInt(1), VSym("x"))
val alias = p
p.setCar(VInt(9))
alias.car
// => 9

alias === p
// => true
@end example

@noindent
This is the price and the point of chapter 3: two names for one mutable
pair behave as one object, and the persistent lists of @ref{0.4} exist
precisely so that the rest of the book never has to reason about such
sharing.

@heading The environment chain, once concretely

The environment structure of @ref{3.2} is a frame of bindings and a parent
link. A definition binds a name in the frame that holds it, and every
holder of that frame observes the new binding.
Walk one chain in the small: a frame @code{inner} extends a frame
@code{global}, and @code{inner} defines @code{x} to 1, so a lookup of
@code{x} through @code{inner} finds 1. Assignment mutates the nearest
frame on the chain that owns the name and never creates a binding: setting
@code{x} to 99 lands in @code{inner}, which holds @code{x}, so a second
lookup through @code{inner} finds 99. @code{global}, which has no @code{x}
of its own, is left without one, and a lookup of @code{x} through
@code{global} fails.

@example kotlin
import arrow.core.Either
import arrow.core.raise.either
import io.kotest.core.spec.style.FunSpec
import io.kotest.matchers.shouldBe
import sicp.runtime.BindingFrame
import sicp.runtime.DatumError
import sicp.runtime.Whole

public class EnvironmentChainTest :
    FunSpec(@{
        test("lookup and assignment follow the chain") @{
            val global = BindingFrame.root()
            val inner = BindingFrame.child(global)
            inner.bind("x", Whole(1L))
            val nested = BindingFrame.child(inner)

            either @{ nested.read("x") @} shouldBe
                Either.Right(Whole(1L))
            either @{ nested.assign("x", Whole(99L)) @} shouldBe
                Either.Right(Unit)
            either @{ inner.read("x") @} shouldBe
                Either.Right(Whole(99L))
            either @{ global.read("x") @} shouldBe
                Either.Left(DatumError.BadDatum("x"))
        @}
    @})
@end example

@noindent
@ref{3.2} presents this chain as an abstract model of what a declaration
or an assignment means; native Kotlin does not execute on a
@code{BindingFrame}, because the compiler resolves names before the
program runs. The chain becomes an executing structure in chapter 4,
where the guest evaluators keep their variables in frames of this shape.
There, @ref{4.3}'s backtracking evaluator does not copy the chain: at each
choice point it saves the value of every cell visible from the current
environment together with a mark in its mutation log, and abandoning a
branch restores those values and undoes the logged writes past the mark.
Ownership, in the sense of Rust's edition, has no Kotlin counterpart: the
collector reclaims unreachable cells, and the book's aliasing arguments are
made with @code{===}, not with lifetimes.

